      "text": "For Structural formula and isomerism, build the answer around carbon’s tetravalency and catenation. First identify the carbon skeleton and then identify the bond type or functional group. Saturated compounds have only single carbon-carbon bonds; alkenes and alkynes are unsaturated and therefore can undergo addition reactions. A homologous series keeps the same functional group while successive members differ by CH₂. In nomenclature questions, count the longest chain, identify the bond type and locate the relevant functional group. In reaction questions, recognise the pattern: combustion with oxygen, oxidation of alcohols, addition to multiple bonds, substitution of alkanes, esterification of an alcohol with a carboxylic acid, and alkaline hydrolysis in saponification. Ethanol and ethanoic acid are especially important because they connect functional groups, reactions and everyday applications. Soap questions require a structural explanation: the hydrophobic hydrocarbon tail associates with grease while the hydrophilic/ionic end interacts with water, enabling micelle formation; hard-water ions can produce insoluble scum with soap. This expanded note is intended for board preparation: learn the principle, write the representative equation, identify the observation or product, and practise applying the same idea to a changed situation. Accuracy matters more than decorative wording. Chemical formulae, subscripts, coefficients, conditions and terminology should be checked before the final answer is written."
